    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35855</id>
    <title>Physicians' Occupational Licensing and the Quantity-Quality Trade-off</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Juan Pablo Atal, Tomás Larroucau, Pablo Muñoz &amp; Cristóbal Otero</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Occupational licensing is a widespread quality regulation that potentially increases the quality of labor but reduces its quantity. We provide a theoretical framework to empirically quantify this trade-off in public hospitals, where both clinical quality and physician shortages are critical concerns. Using quasi-exogenous variation driven by a recent and unprecedented migration of physicians to Chile, together with growth in domestic medical schools, we show that more physicians improve patient outcomes, while lower-quality physicians—as measured by licensing exam performance—worsen them. Incorporating these findings into our theoretical framework, we evaluate the implications of locally changing the stringency of the licensing policy.</content>
